/*]]>*/</style>
<div class=3D"toc-macro rbtoc1758122797794">
<ul class=3D"toc-indentation">
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Architecture">Architectur=
e</a>
<ul class=3D"toc-indentation">
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-JSONLogging">JSON Logging=
</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-LoggingPipeline">Logging =
Pipeline</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Developmentvs.ProductionC=
onsiderations">Development vs. Production Considerations</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-CoreComponents">Core Comp=
onents</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Configuration">Configurat=
ion</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Implementation">Implement=
ation</a>
<ul class=3D"toc-indentation">
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-DynamicallyAdjustingLogLe=
vels">Dynamically Adjusting Log Levels</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-LoadingConfigurationfroma=
File">Loading Configuration from a File</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-LoggingPractices">Logging=
 Practices</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-FieldManagement">Field Ma=
nagement</a></li>
</ul></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-DockerIntegration">Docker=
 Integration</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-ElasticsearchFieldTypes">=
Elasticsearch Field Types</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-DataDictionary">Data Dict=
ionary</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>This logging framework provides structured, flexible, and easily configu=
rable logging for Python applications. It integrates structlog for structur=
ed logs and Python's built-in logging module for precise control, with cust=
om features for runtime configurability across development and production e=
nvironments.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Dynamic Logger Configuration</strong>: Modify log levels and act=
ivate/deactivate specific loggers during runtime</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Structured Logging</strong>: Output logs in JSON format for anal=
ytics platforms like Elasticsearch or Splunk</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Accurate Callsite Information</strong>: Ensure logs accurately r=
eference the calling file, function, and line number</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Custom Log Levels</strong>: Support additional levels, such as T=
RACE, for detailed debugging</p></li>
</ul>
<h1 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Architecture">Architecture</h1>
<h2 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-JSONLogging">JSON Logging</h2>
<p>All AI+ logs will be in JSON format. The industry has widely adopted str=
uctured logging at the application level for several compelling reasons:</p=
>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Reliability</strong>: Application-generated structured logs guar=
antee correct format</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Field consistency</strong>: Developers ensure consistent field n=
aming</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Native nested object support</strong>: Complex objects are handl=
ed appropriately</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Collection tool independence</strong>: Logs maintain structure r=
egardless of collection mechanism</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Structured logging represents a significant advancement over traditional=
 text-based logging by eliminating complex parsing and pre-processing befor=
e ingestion into analytics platforms like Elasticsearch. When applications =
emit plaintext logs, processing pipelines must implement regex patterns, gr=
ok filters, or custom parsers to extract information=E2=80=94approaches tha=
t are brittle, require maintenance when log formats change, and introduce p=
rocessing overhead in high-volume environments.</p>
<p>Our structured JSON logging approach delivers logs to Elasticsearch alre=
ady organized into properly typed fields with consistent naming. This "sche=
ma on write" at the application level avoids the error-prone "schema on rea=
d" approach of traditional logging. The benefits include reduced processing=
 overhead, higher reliability by eliminating potential parsing failures, an=
d immediate analyzability without waiting for pre-processing to complete.</=
p>
<p>Because our application precisely controls its log structure and fields,=
 we gain consistency across all environments=E2=80=94the same log from a lo=
cal development machine, a Docker container, or a Kubernetes pod will have =
identical structure. This consistency is invaluable when building dashboard=
s, alerts, and automated analysis tools that depend on predictable field na=
mes and types.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-LoggingPipeline">Logging Pipeli=
ne</h2>
<p>All logs will be shipped directly from Docker to Fluent Bit using the Fl=
uentd logging driver. This approach offers significant advantages over trad=
itional file-based logging by eliminating the need to manage log file rotat=
ion, compression, archiving, and cleanup processes. The streamlined method =
reduces disk I/O operations, prevents log file buildup, and ensures logs ar=
e immediately available in our analytics platform without delay or data los=
s during rotations.</p>
<p>The opt-in nature of this configuration gives precise control over which=
 containers' logs are collected, allowing filtering of noisy services at th=
e source rather than through post-processing. This architecture also provid=
es a seamless migration path to Kubernetes, where the same logging patterns=
 can be implemented through pod specifications and cluster-wide logging con=
figurations.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Developmentvs.ProductionConside=
rations">Development vs. Production Considerations</h2>
<p>Our implementation previously used pretty-printed JSON for improved cons=
ole readability during development:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">def pre=
tty_print_json(logger, method_name, event_dict):
    """
    Pretty-prints JSON logs to the console for readability.
    """
    event_dict["event"] =3D json.dumps(event_dict["event"], indent=3D4) \
        if isinstance(event_dict["event"], (dict, list)) else event_dict["e=
vent"]
    return event_dict
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>While this approach helps with local debugging, it creates problems when=
 forwarding logs to analytics platforms:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Console logs must be machine-readable=E2=80=94pretty-printed logs with i=
ndentation are difficult to parse</p></li>
<li>
<p>Structured data must remain as objects, not be converted to strings</p><=
/li>
<li>
<p>Field types must maintain consistency across all log messages</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-CoreComponents">Core Components=
</h2>
<p><strong>AiPlusBoundLogger</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Extends structlog's BoundLogger</p></li>
<li>
<p>Dynamically injects metadata including filename, func_name, and lineno i=
nto logs</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Dynamic Configuration</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Configures log levels from a dictionary or file</p></li>
<li>
<p>Allows runtime adjustments via Python's logging hierarchy</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Processors</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>JSONRenderer for structured log output</p></li>
<li>
<p>TimeStamper to add timestamps</p></li>
<li>
<p>StackInfoRenderer for detailed exception stack traces</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>TRACE Log Level</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Adds a TRACE level below DEBUG, ideal for verbose debugging</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Configuration">Configuration</h=
2>
<p>Example Configuration File (logging_config.json):</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">{
    "default_level": "WARNING",
    "loggers": {
        "aiplus": "DEBUG",
        "aiplus.facialRecognition": "INFO",
        "other.module": "ERROR"
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This file defines:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>A default global log level (WARNING)</p></li>
<li>
<p>Specific log levels for individual loggers</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-Implementation">Implementation<=
/h2>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">from lo=
gger import get_logger

logger =3D get_logger("aiplus.facialRecognition")
logger.info("Processing started for facial recognition.")
logger.error("An error occurred while analyzing the image.")
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Output:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">{
    "event": "Processing started for facial recognition.",
    "filename": "example.py",
    "func_name": "&lt;module&gt;",
    "lineno": 7,
    "timestamp": "2024-11-21 17:50:00"
}
{
    "event": "An error occurred while analyzing the image.",
    "filename": "example.py",
    "func_name": "&lt;module&gt;",
    "lineno": 8,
    "timestamp": "2024-11-21 17:50:01"
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-DynamicallyAdjustingLogLevels">=
Dynamically Adjusting Log Levels</h3>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">from lo=
gger import update_logger_level

# Enable TRACE level logging for a specific logger
update_logger_level("aiplus.facialRecognition", "TRACE")

# Disable a logger by setting its level to WARNING
update_logger_level("aiplus.facialRecognition", "WARNING")
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-LoadingConfigurationfromaFile">=
Loading Configuration from a File</h3>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">from lo=
gger import configure_logger, load_config_from_file

# Load logging configuration dynamically
config =3D load_config_from_file("logging_config.json")
configure_logger(config)
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-LoggingPractices">Logging Pract=
ices</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Use structured logging for easy integration with analytics tools</p></li=
>
<li>
<p>Start with high log levels (ERROR or WARNING) in production, enabling lo=
wer levels (DEBUG or TRACE) only when troubleshooting</p></li>
<li>
<p>Dynamically update log levels at runtime to minimize downtime during deb=
ugging</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-FieldManagement">Field Manageme=
nt</h3>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Use constants for field names</strong>: Define field names as co=
nstants to prevent typos and ensure consistency</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Document field types explicitly</strong>: Always include expecte=
d data types to prevent index mapping conflicts</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Review new fields</strong>: All new log fields should be reviewe=
d before being added to production</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Create index templates</strong>: Define Elasticsearch index temp=
lates to set expected field types before data ingestion</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Consider field cardinality</strong>: High-cardinality fields can=
 impact performance</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Use namespacing for component-specific fields</strong>: Prefix f=
ields with component identifiers to avoid collisions</p></li>
</ol>
<h1 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-DockerIntegration">Docker Integ=
ration</h1>
<p>The docker container, either through the runtime or Docker Compose, can =
be configured to send the logs directly to Fluent Bit</p>
<p>While we use Fluent Bit as our log collector, Docker's logging driver is=
 named "fluentd" rather than "fluent-bit." This naming can cause confusion,=
 but it has a logical explanation. The Fluentd protocol was established fir=
st, and Fluent Bit was designed to be compatible with this protocol. When D=
ocker introduced logging drivers, they implemented the "fluentd" driver to =
support the Fluentd forward protocol. This protocol is supported by both Fl=
uentd and Fluent Bit, making them interoperable.</p>
<p>Fluent Bit is a lightweight, resource-efficient alternative to Fluentd d=
esigned specifically for containerized environments and edge computing. Des=
pite the difference in names, both use the same communication protocol, all=
owing us to use Docker's "fluentd" driver to send logs directly to our Flue=
nt Bit service.</p>
<p>Here's an example of how to configure services in a Docker Compose file =
to use the Fluentd logging driver with Fluent Bit:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">version=
: '3'

services:
  facial-recognition:
    image: aiplus/facial-recognition:latest
    logging:
      driver: fluentd
      options:
        fluentd-address: localhost:24224
        tag: docker.facial-recognition
        fluentd-async-connect: "true"
    # Other service configuration...
   =20
  transcribe-summarize:
    image: aiplus/transcribe-summarize:latest
    logging:
      driver: fluentd
      options:
        fluentd-address: localhost:24224
        tag: docker.transcribe-summarize
        labels: "log-level,component"
    labels:
      log-level: info
      component: nlp
    # Other service configuration...
   =20
  fluent-bit:
    image: fluent/fluent-bit:latest
    ports:
      - "24224:24224"
      - "24224:24224/udp"
    volumes:
      - ./fluent-bit.conf:/fluent-bit/etc/fluent-bit.conf
      - ./parsers.conf:/fluent-bit/etc/parsers.conf
    # Other service configuration...
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>With this configuration, container logs are sent directly to Fluent Bit =
running on port 24224 without intermediate file storage. The <code>tag</cod=
e> option provides a namespace for the logs, while <code>labels</code> allo=
ws for additional metadata to be included with each log entry. This approac=
h creates a clean, efficient logging pipeline that eliminates file manageme=
nt overhead while providing rich context for log analysis.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-159:StructuredLoggingFramework-ElasticsearchFieldTypes">Elasti=
csearch Field Types</h1>
<p>During development we encountered an Elasticsearch error:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">"failed=
 to parse field [event] of type [text] in document with id 'tz-JbZUBzsUltXZ=
OvLiM'. Preview of field's value: '{processing_time=3D0.000s, status_code=
=3D404, event=3DHTTP Response, body=3D{detail=3DJob ID not found}}'"</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Originally, our HTTP event logger used a nested structure:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">logger.=
info({
    "event": "HTTP Response",
    "status_code": response.status_code,
    "body": response_body_json,
    "processing_time": f"{process_time:.3f}s",
})
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We refactored to use a flattened structure with consistent field names:<=
/p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">logger.=
info("HTTP Response",
    HttpEventAttributes.HTTP_STATUS_CODE, response.status_code,
    HttpEventAttributes.HTTP_RESPONSE_BODY, response_body_json,
    HttpEventAttributes.PROCESSING_TIME, f"{process_time:.3f}s"
)
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The issue was a <strong>field type collision</strong>. Our application s=
ent an <code>event</code> as a complex object, but Elasticsearch had previo=
usly mapped this field as a text field. Once Elasticsearch creates a mappin=
g for a field, it enforces consistent typing.</p>
<p>Elasticsearch dynamically creates field mappings when it first encounter=
s a field in a document. This process, called "dynamic mapping," automatica=
lly determines the data type based on the first value it sees. For example,=
 if the first document contains <code>"event": "HTTP Request"</code>, Elast=
icsearch will map the <code>event</code> field as a <code>text</code> type.=
 If a subsequent document tries to use that same field name with a differen=
t type (like a nested object), Elasticsearch will reject the document with =
a mapping error.</p>
<p>This =E2=80=9Ctype enforcement=E2=80=9D is a fundamental aspect of Elast=
icsearch's design that ensures data consistency and query predictability. H=
owever, it can cause significant problems in logging systems where differen=
t components might use the same field names differently. Once a field mappi=
ng is established in an index, it cannot be changed without reindexing all =
the data. This "schema on write" approach makes it <strong>critical</strong=
> to plan field structures before data ingestion begins and to maintain a c=
omprehensive data dictionary that all logging components adhere to. For tim=
e-series logging with daily or weekly indices, these collisions might only =
appear periodically as new indices are created, making them particularly di=
fficult to diagnose without understanding Elasticsearch's mapping behavior.=
</p>
<p>This highlights the <strong>critical need for a centralized data diction=
ary</strong> that defines field names, types, and semantics to prevent fiel=
d collisions as different components independently create log fields.</p>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro confluence-information-macro-inf=
ormation">
<span class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info confluence-informa=
tion-macro-icon"></span>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
<p><strong>I cannot stress enough how critical this piece is! We MUST NOT H=
AVE COLLISIONS in an ElasticSearch index. Otherwise WE WILL LOSE DATA.</str=
ong></p>
</div>
</div>
